Handle the publishing work yourself:
1. Connect using the details below and call list_my_mods. Reuse an existing draft or listing only when it is clearly this project; ask if several match. Use search_games and game_info to confirm requirements and install paths.
2. Start from what the project is meant to do: read its README, its notes and its code, and list what a player gets (the games it joins, what they do in it, whether more than one person plays). All of it has to reach the listing. If more than one person plays, getting it listed as multiplayer is part of this job: find how its players connect, set it up as "Playing together" below describes, and host a game once to see Melty pick it up, all before you submit. Then build/package the actual release. Run inspect_package with every archive entry and the relevant small manifests/readmes, review its draftRecipe and fix its findings. Use validate_recipe with entries for each component to check destinations and unmapped files. Then run one_click_check with the recipe, its entries, and says (the project's own feature list, from its README): Melty only publishes mashups that play in one click, and blocks any release that isn't. The same check lists what is still to finish before listing (multiplayer the recipe doesn't set up yet), each with its How: finish every one before you submit. If it says no, fix what it lists (each comes with its fix); if something cannot be fixed, tell me exactly what players would have to do before uploading anything. Include companion launches when the project requires them. Everything a player needs to run it goes in what you upload. When the project needs something made by someone else (a library, a data file, a runtime), get it yourself and package it in the release with credit to its author, as long as its license lets it be shared; when it sits behind a login, I download it once and you take it from there. A loader Melty installs for the game is the exception (game_info lists them): ask for it in the recipe instead. What can't be shared and isn't one of those has to go: change the project so it works without it. The games' own content (models, textures, sounds, levels) is never in the upload either: the mashup reads what it needs from the player's own installed copy of each game when it runs, so every player brings their own, and that is how a mashup uses another game's content. Leave secrets out (passwords, keys, tokens).
3. Use create_mod for a new draft, or update_mod for the chosen existing listing, with the agreed title, tagline, description, games, credits/license and remix choice. When the project is a GitHub repository, pass it as githubRepo: a repository can be on Melty only once, so if create_mod says it is already published, tell me and offer to remix that mashup instead. Record its modId and reuse it when resuming.
4. Upload each finished package with start_upload (actual byte size and SHA-256), PUT its bytes using the returned headers, then finish_upload. Follow upload_part_urls for multipart uploads. Call submit_release with the version, uploadIds and validated recipe; its component fileNames must match the uploads. Fix blocked findings rather than claiming success. When the project is a GitHub repository I can push to, also save the validated recipe as melty.json at its root (a component's fileName may use * where the version goes), and commit it once I agree: Melty then reads the setup straight from the GitHub link, and each new release publishes by itself.
5. Obtain the real gameplay media as agreed above. Use add_screenshot, PUT using the returned headers, then finish_screenshot. Do not publish until this upload is finished.
6. Show me a brief summary of what players will get, required games, multiplayer setup, credits/remix choice and the test result. Then ask whether to publish it, respecting any draft-only or local-only instruction I gave. When I authorize it, call publish: it goes live once Melty's quick safety review passes (usually a couple of minutes). Then call mod_status and open the listing to verify its files and media. Report "live" only when the server confirms it; if it is awaiting review, say so and give me the Studio link. Say it plays in one click only when one_click_check says yes and you tested the install. Once players have it, install_outcomes shows how their installs and launches went, why any failed, how each version of the game did and how playing together went: check it when I ask how the mashup is doing, and fix what it shows.

Playing together (when the project is multiplayer, this is part of getting it listed, not an extra):
A multiplayer project goes up as a multiplayer mashup. Work this out before you submit anything; don't list it as single player and come back to it. Melty gives every player of a multiplayer mashup a join link, lists open games on the mashup's page, and can put a joiner straight into the host's game. Set recipe.multiplayer so it does:
- maxPlayers: the most players one game takes, when the project has a limit. Use the project's real number.
- connect: how Melty connects players without anyone typing an address. It works when the game (or the mod) does two things, so build them in if you are making the mod, or find them if they already exist:
  1. Hosting says where it is. When a game opens to others, it writes its address (a host name or IPv4 address, with a port if needed) to a log file, on a line with fixed words right before it. Tell Melty with connect.address: {log: "{localappdata}/MyMod/logs/latest.log", after: "Hosting at "}. If hosting needs a switch, give it with connect.hostEnv (environment variables) or connect.hostArgs (launch arguments); "{port}" is a free port Melty picks. Melty applies these on every Play.
  2. Joining takes the address at start. Use whichever the game supports: connect.joinArgs (launch arguments), connect.joinEnv (environment variables), connect.joinFile {path, key} (a key=value line in a .properties/.ini/.cfg/.txt file the game reads at start; Melty empties it again for solo play), or connect.joinUri (a steam:// link). "{address}" is the host's address.
  Paths start with a folder token such as {game}, {localappdata} or {minecraft-instance}. Environment variables must be the mod's own (for example MYMOD_JOIN), never system ones.
- If the game can only be joined by hand (an in-game menu), still set recipe.multiplayer with maxPlayers and leave connect out; add connect.address alone if the address appears in a log, so Melty can show it to joiners to paste.
- Players behind home routers cannot reach each other by local address. Use the game's own relay or lobby system, or bundle one the license allows; do not tell players to forward ports.
- game_info says, for the engines Melty knows, how a mod reads who is in a session and the clock they share: start there when the mod has to find its players.
- validate_recipe checks this section. Before saying multiplayer works, test hosting and joining with two running copies (two PCs, or a second instance on this one) and tell me exactly what you tested. If you could not test joining, say so and leave connect out rather than guessing.

